  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers and thunderstorms remain across the \narea today with the potential for areas of heavy rain leading to\na risk of flash flooding. There is also some risk for strong to\nsevere thunderstorms.\n\nAn upper trough and surface low pressure system will move across\nNew York and New England later today and tonight. An associated\nfront will cross the area tonight. These synoptic factors along\nwith the very warm and humid air mass will bring opportunities\nfor showers and thunderstorms today and tonight. \n\nToday, a cold front will be approaching the region. There continues\nto be a signal for ripples of energy along the front. Also, in \nthe upper-levels, a jet streak will be overhead with our area in\nthe right entrance region which allows for additional forcing \nand ascent for our area. Showers and thunderstorms look to be \nongoing into tomorrow morning with an increase in the coverage \nagain during the afternoon and evening.\n\nPWAT values are very high across the area and we've already\nissued several flash flood warnings across the area tonight. \nThe WPC outlook continues with SLIGHT risk (2 of 5) for \nExcessive Rainfall for most areas today. Surrounding the SLIGHT \nrisk is a MARGINAL risk. The Flash flood Watch continues for \nmany areas until 11PM tonight. Total rainfall will range 1 to 3 \ninches in many areas but locally 4 or 5 inches could occur. \n\nIn terms of the severe weather risk today, uncertainty remains \naround the exact extent of instability present due to cloud \ncover and the round of showers/thunderstorms around during the \nmorning. If enough heating develops during the course of the \nday, then this combined with some increase in shear ahead of the\nfront could lead to at least some severe thunderstorm risk. The\nStorm Prediction Center has highlighted an area across New \nJersey and extreme east Pennsylvania for a Marginal risk (level \n1 out of 5) for isolated severe thunderstorms with the primary \nrisk being damaging wind gusts.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A warm front is expected to lift through the region \nTuesday Night into Wednesday, bringing the next chance for showers \nand storms.\n\nOnce the current system gets east of our region, the next thing to \nwatch is a warm front slowly lifting north over our region. The most \nlikely time period for this frontal passage is Tuesday night into \nWednesday, leaving the day time on Tuesday relatively dry. However, \nat least one model is showing a very fast warm frontal passage with \nthe front north of our region by late Wednesday Night. In this fast \nsolution, the pattern is odd, depicting a very pronounced mid and \nupper level short wave ridge in place throughout the day. This \npattern isn't favorable for fast frontal passages. Therefore, have \nfavored the blend of guidance, which favors the slower solution.\n\nAs far as hazards, the primary hazard is likely to be heavy rain \nleading to flash flooding. There will likely be significant low \nlevel moisture advection behind the front, increasing the risk for \nheavy rain. That combined with the fact that storm motions are \nlikely to be quite slow (less than 20 mph), increases the risk for \nflash flooding. One factor which we won't know until late Monday is \nhow much antecedent conditions will play a role in the flash \nflooding threat - areas that get heavy rain through the day on \nMonday will have little opportunity for the water to drain before \nthis next round of rain chances.\n\nThe severe threat is less clear. This will be dependent on the \ntiming of the front - if the frontal passage, and thus prime period \nfor any showers and storms is during the daylight hours when \ninstability is maximized, there will be some threat for damaging \nwinds. Some model soundings show a very dry mid layer, increasing \nthe threat for downbursts. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...For the later half of the week, we will have a warm, \nhumid, and at times stormy pattern in place.\n\nOnce the mid week warm front moves north of our region, we will \nlikely be sandwiched between a Bermuda centered well to our east, \nand a nearly stationary mid level trough axis centered over the \nWestern Great Lakes region.\n\nThe net result of this is that our region will have warm and moist \nsouthwesterly flow in place for multiple days. As such, expect hot \nand humid conditions. Additionally, there could be daily rain \nchances, from two different sources. First, if there is any shower \nor storm development on the sea breeze in the afternoon. Secondly, \nthere could be some embedded very small short wave troughs that \npropagate northeastward over our region during this time.",
